Braces enclose the body of a function, control-flow construct, trait or declared region. In a choice declaration they enclose a comma-separated list of alternatives. Statements in an executable body run in source order until control flow exits or redirects execution.

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    Status = { Ready, Busy }

    report |status Status| {
        if status is {
            case Ready: io.line("ready")
            case Busy: io.line("busy")
        }
    }
]

### Branch scopes

An ordinary `if` encloses both branches in a single pair of braces. The optional bare `else` ends the first branch's scope and starts the second. A nested conditional closes before the enclosing one resumes. A missing closing brace belongs to its opener. A brace inside text cannot close a statement block. Each name declared in a branch stays local to that branch.

A full match starts with `if subject is {`. Each `case` starts a logical line. A colon ends its pattern and optional Boolean guard. The arm's statements can start on the same line or continue on later lines. The next arm at the same depth or the match's closing brace ends its body. A final `else:` handles the remaining cases. A pattern capture belongs to its arm's guard and body.

An expressionless `if {` selects a ready channel operation. Its `case` headers offer sends or receives. A receive binding first becomes available inside the winning body. A final `else:` handles the case where no enabled operation is ready. See @../async (Channels and readiness) for the future runtime contract.

### Producing a result

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    caption |ready Bool| -> String {
        label = if ready {
            then "Ready"
        else
            then "Waiting"
        }
        return label
    }
]

`then` supplies the selected branch's value to the binding. The closing brace resumes execution in the function. `return` sends the result to the caller and exits the function. A brace by itself returns no value.

### Declaration and construction

A name followed by a braced body in executable code declares a lifetime region, as in `scratch { ... }`. See @../memory/#declared-regions (Declared regions) for placement with `into`. Moth has no standalone lexical block or control-flow label.

Square brackets construct values. In a template, a colon separates the head from the body. Braces, semicolons and arm-like words in template text, quoted strings or embedded languages remain literal content. A colon inside a nested template belongs to that template.
